<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Udayagiri and Khandagiri Caves</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Udayagiri and Khandagiri Caves are rock-cut chambers in two neighbouring hills near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhubaneswar">Bhubaneswar</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, carved mostly in the 1st century BCE as dwellings for Jain monks. Many were excavated under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharavela">Kharavela</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga">Kalinga</a> and his queen, and the Hathigumpha ('elephant cave') on Udayagiri bears his long inscription. Their carved friezes are among the earliest narrative sculpture in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/udayagiri-and-khandagiri-caves.jpg" alt="Udayagiri and Khandagiri Caves" width="320" height="152" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Government of Odisha · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Khandagari_and_Udaygiri_featured_image.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Gupta artistic conventions in sculpture and iconography, seen at Sarnath and in the early cave temples of <b>Udayagiri</b>, were transmitted across Southeast Asia through trade and religious contact, shaping the artistic traditions of Cambodia, Thailand, and Java for centuries after the empire itself had dissolved.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The empire's greatest commander was Krishnadevaraya (r. 1509–1529), who defeated the Gajapati rulers of Odisha in campaigns between 1513 and 1519, taking <b>Udayagiri</b> and Kondavidu, and in 1520 defeated Ismail Adil Shah of Bijapur at the Battle of Raichur.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The dynasty is known almost entirely from the Hathigumpha inscription carved in a cave on <b>Udayagiri</b> hill near Bhubaneswar, probably in the 1st century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/mahameghavahana-dynasty">Mahameghavahana dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
